        "sections": [
          {
            "section_key": "overview",
            "title": "What the Big Five describes",
            "render_variant": "rich_text",
            "body_md": "The Big Five describes broad patterns of Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism. A result is a structured reference and working hypothesis, not a diagnosis or fixed identity.",
            "body_html": null,
            "payload_json": null,
            "sort_order": 10,
            "is_enabled": true
          },
          {
            "section_key": "key_concepts",
            "title": "How to read a result",
            "render_variant": "rich_text",
            "body_md": "Each dimension is continuous, and the five dimensions should be read together. Interpretation depends on context, time, language, and the instrument used. Different instruments and facet systems are not interchangeable.",
            "body_html": null,
            "payload_json": null,
            "sort_order": 20,
            "is_enabled": true
          },
          {
            "section_key": "why_it_matters",
            "title": "Use in work and learning contexts",
            "render_variant": "callout",
            "body_md": "Trait patterns can support questions about preferred pace, feedback, collaboration, and work environment. Use them only as supplementary context alongside interests, skills, values, constraints, and real-world exploration; they do not determine occupational fit or outcomes.",
            "body_html": null,
            "payload_json": {
              "career_claim_mode": "supplementary_explanation_only",
              "recommendation_authority": false
            },
            "sort_order": 30,
            "is_enabled": true
          },
          {
            "section_key": "who_should_read",
            "title": "Evidence and limits",
            "render_variant": "rich_text",
            "body_md": "Evidence base:\n- Goldberg (1990), Big-Five factor structure: https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.59.6.1216\n- Soto and John (2017), BFI-2 hierarchical model: https://doi.org/10.1037/pspp0000096\n\nThese sources support broad trait structure and one hierarchical model. They do not validate FermatMind scores or predict a person’s health, career, admission, hiring, income, or future.",
            "body_html": null,
            "payload_json": null,
            "sort_order": 40,
            "is_enabled": true
          }
        ],
